    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d302">

                <pb facs="0351" n="277" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v01/pg277"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/13319</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Denmark</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Egan</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-10-23T13:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 23, 1917, 1 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received October 24, 3 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1464. During a conversation with an official Austrian temporarily in Copenhagen,
                    I asked: “Why do you not surrender, then also food troubles would come to an
                    end?” Instead of receiving the suggestion with disdain he replied simply: “We
                    cannot surrender. The <pb facs="0352" n="278" xml:id="pg278"/> Italians want
                    Trieste and Fiume and the entire littoral to cut us off from the sea, to which
                    we could never consent.”</p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
